2493: \begin{proposition}[Current-package acceptance and certificate ledger]
2494: \label{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}
2495: Let $P^{+}$ be the finite current package consisting of the primary source
2496: \path{submission_abstract.tex}, this supplement \path{main.tex}, and, under the
2497: exact-or-archive-equivalent support-surface condition of
2498: Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition},
2499: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, together with
2500: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
2501: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
2502: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
2503: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json},
2504: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py},
2505: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}, and the explicit
2506: historical paths named in the case catalogue.  Root-level diagnostics such as
2507: \path{PIPELINE.md}, \path{SOURCE_MAP.md}, \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}, and
2508: \path{source_interface_record.json} may explain local provenance or path-verified
2509: history, but they are not finite package coordinates for this proposition unless
2510: their contents are represented in \path{main.tex} or in the submitted support
2511: surface.  The TeX source fixes the displayed six-row ledger.  This state is denoted
2512: $P^{+}_{6}$ in Theorem~\ref{thm:nested-package-states}: it is the row-frozen
2513: certificate state for $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, before the submitted manual theorem-ledger
2514: coordinate $a_7$ is adjoined.  The review-bundle files in this definition are
2515: supplemental-record components: claims that depend on them are conditional on
2516: those files being submitted with matching paths and records, or on an archival
2517: equivalent satisfying Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}.
2518: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2519: and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} form a finite submission-local
2520: acceptance-entry ledger for the load-bearing current-package claims used in
2521: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}.  For each entry
2522: $a_i$, the two tables determine the certificate fields
2523: \[
2524: \mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)
2525: \]
2526: and each such certificate is locally checkable at the path-verified evidence
2527: level.  No load-bearing claim outside these six entries is accepted by the
2528: current-package instantiation.
2529: \end{proposition}
2530: 
2531: \begin{proof}
2532: The acceptance-entry ledger is finite because Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2533: has six rows.  Its rows enumerate exactly the assertions on which the current
2534: package corollary depends: the bounded imported source interface, the existence
2535: of the presentation-only package, and the four case-study lessons for intake
2536: isolation, overlap blocking, fake-extension rejection, and artifact limitation.
2537: The third column assigns the claim-kind set $K_i$ for each accepted assertion.
2538: These kinds all lie in the seven-kind domain of the dispatch function $\Gamma$.
2539: 
2540: For each row, Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger} supplies the remaining
2541: certificate fields.  The declared evidence level $\ell_i$ is path-verified in
2542: every row.  The durable manuscript contribution is the finite row text printed in
2543: the tables and the proof that these fields have the required schema shape.  The
2544: matching assertions about JSON mirrors, pass-record files, manifest entries, and
2545: verifier logs are supplemental-record assertions and require the named
2546: \path{review_bundle/} files to be present.  The evidence surface $E_i$ is a finite
2547: list of submission-local files, review-bundle records, pinned source-interface
2548: records, or explicit historical paths.  The promotion field $H_i$ is either the current active-paper marker
2549: \path{main.tex} together with \path{PIPELINE.md}, or the explicit marker that the
2550: case row does not itself cross a new route boundary.  The pass-record field
2551: $R_i$ names the finite gate records used by the row: source-interface extraction
2552: and consistency for $a_1$, package and submission-pack records for $a_2$,
2553: promotion for $a_3$, overlap and submission-state for $a_4$, theorem-content and
2554: discovery gating for $a_5$, and artifact status plus limitation-ledger gating for
2555: $a_6$.
2556: 
2557: The discovery field behaves exactly as required by the certificate schema.  It is
2558: present only for $a_5$, the row whose accepted claim has discovery kind, and it
2559: names the pinned source-interface rows that supply the certificate-and-ledger,
2560: demotion, lineage, pipeline-safety, and main-pipeline witnesses.  For the other
2561: rows the claim-kind set does not contain discovery, so $D_i$ is absent.  Finally,
2562: the boundary field $B_i$ records the same limitation in every row: the package is
2563: using path-verified evidence, not command-run evidence, unless the command,
2564: source commit, environment, exit code, and log path are explicitly recorded.
2565: Consequently the ledger is historical and path-verified; it does not assert a
2566: fresh Lean build, daemon pass, full-source rerun, Rule110 or GroundCompiler
2567: rerun, artifact-suite validation, or external venue acceptance.  Thus each row
2568: determines a locally checkable publication certificate
2569: \(\mathcal C(a_i)\).
2570: 
2571: It remains only to see that the ledger is complete for the current-package
2572: instantiation.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}
2573: uses no unlisted positive claim: its source-interface premise is $a_1$, its
2574: package premise is $a_2$, and its four bounded case-study premises are
2575: $a_3$--$a_6$.  Any proposed stronger reading, such as a fresh full-source rebuild,
2576: publication-daemon run, Rule110 dynamic rerun, artifact-suite semantic
2577: validation, unledgered discovery claim, or external venue acceptance, has no
2578: acceptance entry in Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and also violates
2579: the boundary field in Table~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}.  By
2580: Theorems~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} and
2581: \ref{thm:evidence-level-overclaim-obstruction}, such a reading is therefore
2582: rejected by a finite obstruction subrecord rather than accepted by this
2583: package.\end{proof}
2584: 
2585: \subsection{Current-package instantiation}
2586: 
2587: \noindent\emph{Six-row and seven-row states.}
2588: The current-package certificate state in this subsection is the six-row state
2589: $P^{+}_{6}$ with acceptance rows \path{a1}--\path{a6}.  The later
2590: theorem-obligation integration in
2591: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary} is the seven-row extension
2592: $P^{+}_{7}$, obtained from $P^{+}_{6}$ by adding exactly the manual submitted
2593: theorem-ledger coordinate \path{a7}.  Thus the two theorems describe nested support states,
2594: not competing current-package mirrors.
2595: 
2596: \begin{theorem}[Current-package schema-relative finite-record certificate exactness]
2597: \label{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}
2598: For the six-row finite current-package state $P^{+}_{6}$ of
2599: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}, assume the
2600: support-surface availability condition of
2601: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}: the submitted supplemental
2602: material contains the exact \path{review_bundle/} directory with
2603: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, with refreshed digest rows and
2604: rerun finite-verifier logs for every named bundle coordinate regenerated after
2605: the digest surface was written.  With that support
2606: surface present, the six ledger entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ as printed in
2607: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}
2608: and as checked by \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log} are
2609: exactly the accepted schema-relative finite-record instantiation of the finite-record interface theorem,
2610: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  More precisely, the package map
2611: \[
2612: a_i\longmapsto \mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)
2613: \]
2614: is a bijection from the accepted current-package claim rows printed in this
2615: source to the locally checkable certificates accepted by the submitted schema
2616: verifier at the declared path-verified evidence level, relative only to the
2617: finite fields read by that schema.  The assigned-gate rows are sound only in the
2618: finite-record sense of
2619: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}: they certify assigned
2620: gate coverage and declared evidence pointers, while any stronger assertion of
2621: independent gate implementation soundness would require source paths, commands,
2622: source states, environments, exit codes, and log paths for those gates.  Without
2623: that support surface, the theorem retains only the printed source-level inventory
2624: of six intended certificate rows
2625: and not the machine-readable current-package acceptance claim.  The durable TeX source
2626: fixes the six row statements and their negative boundaries; the supplemental
2627: record supplies the machine-readable mirror and verifier log only if it travels
2628: with the submission or with an archival replacement satisfying
2629: the final-support closure condition just stated.  Every
2630: current-package reading requiring a seventh accepted row, a different claim kind,
2631: a stronger evidence level, an additional gate pass record, a fresh command-run
2632: tuple, or an external venue decision is outside this bijection and is rejected by
2633: the finite obstruction skeleton and, more sharply, by the positive extension-budget
2634: criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The exactness
2635: is therefore row-frozen, finite-record, and verifier-log bounded: editing any of
2636: the six rows or any machine-readable mirror changes the finite package unless the
2637: certificate verifier is rerun and the displayed tables and log are updated
2638: together.
2639: \end{theorem}
2640: 
2641: \begin{proof}
2642: \emph{Construction from the displayed rows.}
2643: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger} constructs,
2644: for each row $a_i$ of Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}
2645: and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}, a certificate
2646: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\).  The row set is finite and
2647: has exactly six elements.  The construction is injective because the row
2648: identifier, accepted claim text, and evidence surface in
2649: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} are part of the displayed record data;
2650: two different rows cannot have the same accepted current-package claim row.  It
2651: is surjective onto the accepted current-package certificates because the same
2652: proposition states that no load-bearing claim outside these six entries is
2653: accepted by the current-package instantiation.
2654: 
2655: \emph{Support-surface instantiation.}
2656: Conditioned on the supplemental records being present, matching the displayed
2657: paths, and matching the final digest manifest
2658: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md},
2659: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records} supplies the finite
2660: schema instance over \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
2661: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
2662: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
2663: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, the verifier script, and the
2664: recorded verifier log.  The recorded log states command
2665: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, source commit
2666: \path{automath repository commit 305ee93353b16babc461bfad1a7aee9c808ee268 plus final package digest manifest review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, final package digest manifest
2667: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, environment \path{Python 3.10.11 on Windows 10}, exit code \path{0}, and log path
2668: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  The companion logs
2669: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log} and
2670: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-05.log} likewise
2671: record exit code \path{0} against the same final digest manifest.  That instance checks kind-domain membership,
2672: path-verified evidence-level membership, assigned-gate coverage, the five
2673: pass-record fields, discovery-field discipline, and the path-verified command
2674: boundary for entries $a_1,\ldots,a_6$.  Hence every certificate in the image of
2675: the displayed package map is accepted by the submitted schema verifier at exactly
2676: the declared path-verified level when that supplemental record surface is part of
2677: the review package.  The support surface is finite:
2678: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure} later proves that the bundle-backed
2679: component for this package is admissible exactly when the exact
2680: \path{review_bundle/} directory is submitted with its final digest manifest.
2681: Without that finite surface,
2682: the TeX proof still supplies the finite schema classification of the displayed
2683: rows, but not the additional claim that the submitted executable mirror and log
2684: were available to the reviewer.  By
2685: Theorem~\ref{thm:assigned-gate-soundness-audit-boundary}, the same verification
2686: does not audit the implementation internals of the named gates: it verifies the
2687: presence and local evidence surfaces of the assigned finite pass rows, and a
2688: fresh implementation-soundness reading would be a command-result escalation not
2689: present in these six certificates.
2690: 
2691: \emph{Maximality and non-escalation.}
2692: Conversely, a stronger reading changes at least one finite component of the
2693: record: it adds a row, changes $K_i$, changes $\ell_i$, adds a required gate in
2694: $R_i$, replaces the path-verified boundary $B_i$ by a command-run tuple, or adds
2695: an external venue-decision surface.  Such a record is not in the six-row image
2696: just identified.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, a
2697: genuine strengthening must pay a positive finite extension budget in added
2698: coordinates, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton},
2699: the first unpaid coordinate gives a bad subrecord: a missing evidence surface,
2700: missing gate record, missing promotion or venue field, missing discovery-source
2701: row, or unsupported command-run escalation.  Thus the six certificates are not a
2702: sample of an unrecorded full-pipeline execution; they are exactly the row-frozen,
2703: finite, path-verified package instantiation of
2704: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.\end{proof}
2705: 
2706: \begin{corollary}[Row-frozen current-package invariance]
2707: \label{cor:row-frozen-current-package-invariance}
2708: Under the support-surface hypotheses of
2709: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the current-package
2710: acceptance decision factors through the six displayed rows
2711: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) and their certificate fields only.  Any two ambient repository,
2712: daemon, artifact, or venue states that induce the same six tuples
2713: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the same submitted support
2714: surface, and the same verifier log path induce the same accepted current-package
2715: certificate set.  Conversely, a reading that depends on a seventh row, a modified
2716: row, a fresh command execution, semantic validity of BEDC/Lean/Rule110 artifacts,
2717: implementation soundness of the verifier or gates, upload status, or venue
2718: decision is not invariant under this six-row projection and is accepted only
2719: after it is added as a finite extension coordinate and rechecked.  Thus the
2720: current-package theorem is exactly a row-frozen finite-record theorem, not a
2721: current-execution reproducibility theorem and not a semantic-validity theorem.
2722: \end{corollary}
2723: 
2724: \begin{proof}
2725: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} identifies the accepted
2726: current-package certificates with the bijective image of the six rows
2727: \(a_1,\ldots,a_6\) under the map
2728: \(a_i\mapsto \mathcal C(a_i)\), conditioned on the submitted support surface and
2729: the recorded verifier log.  The verifier then reads only the finite fields of
2730: these tuples, the fixed dispatch table, and the cited submitted surfaces.  By
2731: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, two ambient states
2732: with identical values on those finite coordinates have the same verifier
2733: decision and the same finite certificate set.  This proves the stated invariance.
2734: 
2735: For the converse boundary, each listed stronger reading changes a coordinate not
2736: contained in the six-row projection: a seventh accepted row changes the row set;
2737: a modified row changes one of \(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i\); a fresh command
2738: execution changes the boundary field to command-run evidence; semantic validity
2739: or implementation soundness adds claim kinds, evidence surfaces, and gate rows;
2740: upload or venue status adds external route/submission fields.  By
2741: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, such a genuine
2742: strengthening has a positive finite extension budget, and by
2743: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} an unpaid coordinate is
2744: reported as a finite bad subrecord.  Hence the six-row exactness result cannot be
2745: read as a claim about fresh reproducibility or semantic validity; those readings
2746: require an enlarged record and a new acceptance check.\end{proof}
2747: 
2748: \begin{theorem}[Current-package semantic non-escalation]
2749: \label{thm:current-package-semantic-non-escalation}
2750: Let $P^{+}_{6}$ be the six-row current-package state of
2751: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, and assume the same
2752: support-surface availability condition.  For each accepted row $a_i$ with
2753: certificate
2754: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\), the following are
2755: equivalent for any proposed stronger reading $a_i^{+}$ of the same manuscript
2756: row.
2757: \begin{enumerate}
2758: \item $a_i^{+}$ is accepted by the finite-record safety interface for the current
2759: package.
2760: \item The package supplies a positive extension record
2761: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) of the normal form in
2762: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}, and every nonempty
2763: coordinate of that record lies on the submitted support surface and passes its
2764: assigned gates.
2765: \end{enumerate}
2766: Consequently no row of $P^{+}_{6}$ entails, by itself, semantic correctness of
2767: BEDC source theory, Lean, Rule110/GroundCompiler, the certificate-verifier
2768: implementation, upload surfaces, or venue decisions.  In the current submission
2769: those stronger readings are certifiable only as extension claims; when the
2770: extension coordinates are absent they are rejected by a finite bad subrecord, and
2771: the accepted content remains exactly the six row-frozen finite-record certificates
2772: of Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.
2773: \end{theorem}
2774: 
2775: \begin{proof}
2776: Fix $i$.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, the row
2777: $a_i$ is accepted exactly through the finite tuple
2778: \(\mathcal C(a_i)=(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)\) at the declared
2779: path-verified level, and the theorem identifies no additional accepted
2780: current-package row outside the six-row image.  If $a_i^{+}$ is accepted as a
2781: properly stronger reading, then it is not record-equivalent to $a_i$ in the
2782: preorder of Definition~\ref{def:stronger-reading-preorder}.  Hence
2783: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form} collects the finite
2784: differences into a positive extension record
2785: \(\Delta(a_i,a_i^{+})\) consisting of added claim kinds, evidence surfaces,
2786: gate-pass rows, boundary fields, discovery rows, or command-run data.  Acceptance
2787: forces each added coordinate to satisfy the corresponding schema clause A1--A6,
2788: so the extension coordinates must be submitted and gate-passing.  This proves
2789: (1)$\Rightarrow$(2).
2790: 
2791: Conversely, suppose the positive extension record is supplied on the submitted
2792: surface and every added coordinate passes its assigned gates.  Enlarging
2793: \(\mathcal C(a_i)\) by those coordinates gives the certificate tuple displayed in
2794: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-extension-budget-normal-form}.  The six schema clauses of
2795: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} then hold for the
2796: enlarged reading, and Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} accepts
2797: $a_i^{+}$ exactly at the declared evidence level.  This proves (2)$\Rightarrow$(1).
2798: 
2799: The listed semantic readings are all proper strengthenings of a six-row finite
2800: certificate: BEDC and Lean semantic correctness add formal-source or
2801: implementation-soundness coordinates; Rule110/GroundCompiler semantic validity
2802: adds artifact-semantic or evaluator-run coordinates; verifier implementation
2803: soundness adds implementation source, command, and pass records; upload and venue
2804: readings add external support-surface or decision fields.  None of those
2805: coordinates is one of the six row-frozen certificates identified by
2806: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}.  If the corresponding
2807: extension record is absent,
2808: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton} returns the first
2809: missing evidence surface, gate record, external boundary, discovery row, or
2810: command-run tuple as a finite bad subrecord.  Therefore the current-package
2811: exactness theorem is a finite-record certificate theorem and not a semantic
2812: correctness theorem for the named systems or surfaces.\end{proof}
2813: 
2814: \begin{corollary}[Current package path-verified instantiation]
2815: \label{cor:current-package-path-verified-instantiation}
2816: For the current presentation package, let $P$ be the finite package consisting
2817: of the primary source \path{submission_abstract.tex}, this supplement
2818: \path{main.tex}, and the exact-or-archive-equivalent review-bundle support
2819: surface of Proposition~\ref{audit:review-bundle-availability-condition}, together
2820: with the executable certificate records named in
2821: Proposition~\ref{prop:machine-readable-certificate-records}.  Root-level workflow
2822: diagnostic files are local historical/path-verified context only unless their
2823: records are represented on that submitted surface.  The path catalogue
2824: in $P$, through the exact six-row certificate map of
2825: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness}, instantiates
2826: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, at the
2827: path-verified evidence level as follows.  The imported discovery-gate interface
2828: is fixed by the pinned newmath commit
2829: \path{3fb3d6a0641767388a401883062aa522ea0b397b}, the source-interface table,
2830: the JSON mirror, and the bundled source snapshot; the four case-study records
2831: are bounded witnesses for promotion, route-overlap/submission-state,
2832: theorem-content, and artifact-limitation gates; and every such case record is
2833: usable only for the bounded manuscript lesson named in its row.  The finite
2834: acceptance entries and certificate fields are exactly the rows of
2835: Tables~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} and~\ref{tab:current-certificate-ledger}.
2836: Therefore the
2837: current package supports exactly the path-verified publication-safety claim that
2838: its load-bearing case-study assertions are reviewable, gate-typed, and equipped
2839: with locally checkable publication certificates at the path-verified level.  The
2840: corollary is historical and record-level: it instantiates the certificate
2841: constructor using the listed paths, tables, and bundle records, not by rerunning
2842: the whole workflow.  It does not instantiate any stronger claim that the full
2843: newmath source tree, a publication daemon, an artifact validator, or the Rule110
2844: dynamic artifact suite was freshly rerun.
2845: \end{corollary}
2846: 
2847: \begin{proof}
2848: The package $P$ contains exactly the submission-local source files and manifest
2849: records named in the statement.  Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness}
2850: shows that the source-interface table is the complete imported interface used by
2851: this paper, and Propositions~\ref{prop:reviewable-source-snapshot-interface}
2852: and \ref{prop:source-interface-record-file-consistency} identify the same
2853: interface by pinned commit, JSON mirror, and bundled source snapshot.  Hence the
2854: discovery-gate hypotheses of the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} are
2855: not supplied by free prose; they are supplied, at the bounded source-interface
